\par\par \b \uc2  POLISH ZLOTY TO STAY STRONG AS GOVERNMENT FORMS. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wojciech Moskwa \par \uc2 563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Oct 10 (Reuter) - The zloty is seen holding strong next week with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's upside carefully kept hazy by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as foreign investors return to Poland amid an ever-clearer political situation, analysts said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that foreign capital would continue to slowly seep into high-yielding Polish treasuries next week despite this week's zloty gains and some remaining political risk. \par \par \uc2  Next week Poland's new prime minister should be announced, but markets already welcomed the liberal Freedom Union's (UW) expected leading role in guiding the country's finances in the soon-to-be-formed coalition with the larger Solidarity group. \par \par \uc2  "Foreigners will stay interested in Polish paper in the near-term despite a strong zloty...as positive signals, such as the UW's large role in shaping economic policy, continue to appear," said Robert Gluch, dealer at ING Bank in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said foreign investors would again be present at Monday's 1.5 billion zloty T-bill auction despite the zloty hitting another three-month high on Friday at 1.13 percent above the trading band centre. \par \par \uc2  Foreign inflows into Polish fixed bonds and bills fueled much of the zloty's 0.6 point rise this week. \par \par \uc2  The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBP) had refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  agaist the zloty for most of the week, creating more uncertainty about the zloty's short-term movements. \par \par \uc2  "The zloty should test higher grounds next week, even approch 1.5 percent despite some expected profit taking, unless the NBP \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  and knocks it down," said Witold Wozniak, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Deutsche Bank Polska SA. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the NBP should keep the market guessing about the zloty's ceiling next week, with some expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the zloty between 1.3 and 1.5 percent and others expecting the bank to step in only after the market stabilised. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said yields would probably hold flat at Monday's T-bill tender with investor focus on the benchmark 52-week bill, which yielded 22.88 percent at this Monday's auction. \par \par \uc2  "The benchmark should stay flat or it could even inch up a fraction on Monday as the large 900 million zloty offer absorbs much interest," said Artur Wrobel, dealer at Bank Handlowy SA. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the yield of the shortest 8-week paper should slip to around 22.55 percent from the previous 22.62, while the 13-week yield would hold flat around 22.85 percent. The yield of the least popular 26-week paper was seen around 23.10. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said next year's budget will show whether the next government, formed under the trade union-led Solidarity alliance, would carry out needed fiscal tightening in 1998. \par \par \uc2  "If next year's budget is tight, with a deficit around 1.5 percent of gross domestic product, then another foreign investor run for Polish paper can be expected," said Rafal Petch, dealer at Bank of America in London. \par \par \uc2  Economists have long said Poland must adjust its fiscal account to match its restrictive monetary policy and keep the country's external deficit from triggering a full-blown economic crisis similar to that of the Czech Republic. \par \par \uc2  -- Warsaw Newsroom +48 22 653 9700 \par \par \uc2  E-mail: warsaw.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par 