\par\par \b \uc2  Poland's Zloty -2:  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  May Prefer Volatility \b0\par\par\uc2 255 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 1997\par 11:19\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Malgorzata Sawicka, a dealer at Bank Handlowy, said the level of 0.5% above parity could trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. 'The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will undoubtedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' she said, noting it had been monitoring the market carefully. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said turnover was high and spreads were wide, reflecting uncertainty about the appropriate level of the zloty. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said that uncertainty and volatility are precisely what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to see. Volatility increases the risk for foreign investors and limits inflows of short-term, speculative capital. \par \par \uc2  In recent remarks on the zloty, National Bank of Poland President Hanna Gronkiewicz-Waltz has suggested that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will give the market its head. \par \par \uc2  'The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will allow for greater volatility on the market,' said Bogdan Stepkowski, a dealer at ING Bank. 'It's hard to speak of an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level.' \par \par \uc2  The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the parity rate for the dollar at 3.4317 zlotys and for the mark at 1.9374 zlotys. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 0.36% below its parity rate, and the mark was at 0.11% above its parity rate. \par \par \uc2  The Polish zloty is fixed to a basket of five Western \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is devalued at a rate of 1% per month. It is a