\par\par \b \uc2  Polish zloty creeps lower, looks to NBP for help. \b0\par\par\uc2 381 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, July 17 (Reuter) - The Polish zloty crept lower against its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket on Thursday as news of another wave of heavy rains and floods expected this weekened did little to stop fleeing foreign portfolio investors, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said the zloty's decline was limited because banks were wary of a National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (NBP) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lift the zloty after the finance ministry and the IMF both signalled that the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide has been excessive. \par \par \uc2  "The zloty is being pushed down by exiting foreigners and the market is waiting for the NBP to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and potentially pull the zloty back up," said Tomasz Wolosz, dealer at BRE SA. \par \par \uc2  The zloty closed at -3.78/-4.23 percent from the trading band centre, against Wednesday's -2.73/-4.24 percent. \par \par \uc2  It closed at 3.4900/5100 to the dollar against 3.4550/050 on Wedneday and at 1.9500/50 to the mark against 1.9285/575. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Marek Belka said the recent zloty selling trend should be reversed, but the market remained bearish on the local curency, which lost five percent over the last week, and kept the local unit gradually headed downward on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The money market relaxed slightly after several days of nearly no liquidity and rates slipped from levels nearly reaching the 25 percent lombard rate, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Overnight rates closed at 22.80/23.20 percent, down from opening's 23.65/23.85 percent and tomorrow/next at 22.90/23.30 percent from 23.70/23.90. \par \par \uc2  Earlier today in a special session on flood relief funding, the parliament passed an amendment to the 1997 budget which increased by 2.5 billion zlotys the amount of treasuries the government can sell to the NBP. \par \par \uc2  "This means the NBP will have to sterilise large amounts of cash, pulling up market rates, and it creates some room for an official interest rate rise," said Robert Gluch, dealer at ING bank in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could opt for an official interest rates rise because an inflow of hot foreign capital was not expected amid bearish zloty sentiment. \par \par \uc2  -- Wojciech Moskwa +48 22 653 9700 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt7h02fn0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
