\par\par \b \uc2  Polish finmin calm on zloty fall, expects rebound. \b0\par\par\uc2 232 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, July 17 (Reuter) - Polish Finance Minister Marek Belka said the zloty's recent sharp fall was caused by speculation rather than bad fundamentals and it should start to firm again soon. \par \par \uc2  He said in a radio interview on Thursday that the zloty's rise on Wednesday, which followed dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 , proved its earlier losses were unfounded. \par \par \uc2  "It was not foreign investors, it seems, who were fleeing the zloty but domestic traders who went wild and began moving from zlotys to dollars. I watch it with calm...I think it will now reverse," he declared. \par \par \uc2  The zloty plunged by five percent on Tuesday against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a fixing of 5.69 percent below the parity rate, It rebounded to 3.41 percent below parity on Wednedsay after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $5 million on the market. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the fall was propelled by fears, both in Poland and abroad, that the government's flood relief spending would boost domestic demand and imports, widening an already big trade deficit and possibly increasing inflation. \par \par \uc2  Heavy rains in southern Poland have caused floods which put more than 4,000 sq km under water, killed at least 39 people and forced tens of thousands from their homes. \par \par \uc2  -- Warsaw Newsroom +48 22 653 9700 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt7h02fkh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
