\par\par \b \uc2  FOREIGNERS SEEN FLOCKING BACK TO ZLOTY NEXT WEEK. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wojciech Moskwa \par \uc2 584 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, May 30 (Reuter) - Foreign investors are expected to flock back to high yielding zloty-denominated papers next week after a brief but heavy-handed withdrawal over the past days pushed the zloty down to its lowest levels ever, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors, either covering losses caused by the Czech crown's sharp fall or cutting Central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure in general, pulled out some $400 million from Poland over the last week, sinking the zloty more than two percent. \par \par \uc2  But on Thursday and Friday the zloty regained initiative and charged upward, backed by bullish statements from Poland's Finance Minister and renewed foreign interest in Polish papers. \par \par \uc2  "After several weeks of minimal interest, foreign investors are likely to again show up in force at Monday's T-bill tender ...(and) put the zloty under upward pressure next week," said Wlodzimierz Kado, dealer at Bank Handlowy SA. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said, however, that the zloty's upward push would probably meet National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  against it, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday when the zloty strengthened above 1.5 percent from parity. \par \par \uc2  "Today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed the NBP believes 1.5 percent would be a good resistance, but it may well have to repeat its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -buying in order to enforce this level," said Mateusz Bieniek, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Societe Generale in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  Some other dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 1.5 percent from parity may have been made simply to cool the zloty, which soared more than one percent against the basket in several hours of trading, not to establish new resistance. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, the NBP bought some $20 million between 1.62 and 1.47 percent from the parity rate, capping the zloty's gains. \par \par \uc2  The zloty closed the week at 1.53/1.47 percent from the parity rate, up from last Friday's close at 0.64/0.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  It briefly sank on Tuesday to an all-time low against the basket, level with parity, as the Czech crown tumbled some seven to eight percent amid foreign investor withdrawal. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that more upbeat Polish fundamentals, such as steady economic growth created by largely restructured industry, and tighter regulations over capital markets, kept the zloty from plunging in line with its Czech neighbour. \par \par \uc2  At Monday's 1.6 billion zloty primary T-bill auction yields are likely to hold unchanged or edge upward, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Yields should remain on the same level, or nudge upward on the longest bills, as the treasury increased its offer," said Marta Kepa, dealer at Pekao SA. \par \par \uc2  The maximum yield of the benchmark 52-week T-bill was expected to be around 21.48 percent, against a 21.40 percent average yield at Monday's tender. \par \par \uc2  Yields of 26-week, 13-week and eight-week papers were expected to hold steady around 21.34, 20.87 and 20.77 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that even though the recent foreign sales had absorbed some demand from domestic corporates, Polish banks were still likely to bid for large amounts of bills on Monday, as a new monthly reserve period pulled down one-day rates. \par \par \uc2  "Costs of financing will probably drop next week as a new monthly reserve period begins, so banks may try to buy bills for their trading portfolios," said Robert Gluch, dealer at ING Bank in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  -- Warsaw Newsroom +48 22 653 9700 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020040130dt5u018fj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
