\par\par \b \uc2  Polish zloty rebounds as minister lifts confidence. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wojciech Moskwa \par \uc2 398 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 1997\par 15:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, May 28 (Reuter) - The Polish zloty started to rebound on Wednesday after falling more than two percent over the last four trading days in sympathy with the plunging Czech crown, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  got a boost from Wednesday comments by Finance Minister Marek Belka said the main wave of foreign investor withdrawal from zloty instruments has ended. \par \par \uc2  "Belka signalled that there was no fundamental reason for keeping the zloty weak and a ( hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) selling spree ensued," said Piotr Czyzewski, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at ING bank in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  "More gains could be in store for the zloty," he added. \par \par \uc2  At 1200 GMT the zloty rebounded to 1.19/1.04 percent stronger than the parity rate, up from 0.47/0.32 percent at opening and Tuesday's intraday lows dipping to parity. \par \par \uc2  It was at 3.2040/00 to the dollar and at 1.8875/95 to the mark, against opening's 3.2285/50 and 1.8990/20. \par \par \uc2  Last Thursday and Friday the zloty dropped two percent against the basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as foreign investors sold the Polish unit to cut their central European exposure after the sharp fall of the Czech crown. \par \par \uc2  Belka estimated that a total of $1.2 billion of short-term speculative capital was in Poland, which he said was not enough to significantly weaken the zloty even if all of it pulled out. \par \par \uc2  "I estimate that even if all the short-term, speculative capital left Poland, it would only have a minor effect on the zloty exchange rate," Belka told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  But he said he did not know how much foreign short-term capital flowed out of Poland during the pullout but quoted press reports which said the sales totalled some $400 million. \par \par \uc2  He also said the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's small \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week to stop the zloty's fall proved very effective and made him confident the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in control of the situation. \par \par \uc2  Last Friday the NBP bought an estimated $15 million which stopped the zloty in its tracks at just above the parity rate. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that Poland's economy, which was in a much better shape than the Czech Republic's, was an unlikely environment for a zloty plunge similar to that of the crown. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt5s04qmu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
