\par\par \b \uc2  NBP weakens bullish zloty, one-day depos fall. \b0\par\par\uc2 448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, April 21 (Reuter) - The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the zloty for the third day running on Monday to stop its rise fuelled by large-scale foreign investor entry at the primary T-bill tender, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Delears estimated foreign bids at the 1.3 billion zloty auction at $350 million, a part of which found its way onto the interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, propelling the zloty past NBP-accepted levels of around 2.5 pecent from the trading band centre. \par \par \uc2  "The zloty charged on foreign bids at the primary tender, but once the NBP bought all that foreigners wanted to sell, the market calmed down," said Tomasz Nowakowski of Bank Handlowy. \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought some $40 million on the market, bringing the zloty down to 2.46/2.43 percent from the parity rate from around 2.52 percent when it first stepped in. \par \par \uc2  The zloty closed at 3.1300/14 to the dollar, up from 3.1385/05 on Friday and at 1.8365/71 to the mark, down from 1.8275/80. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market might see more NBP action tomorrow as some foreigners, who bought an estimated $250 million at the primary T-bill tender after bidding for more than $350 million, may exchange \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  into zlotys to pay for the bills. \par \par \uc2  "Tomorrow may again be nervous because some of those who bought the bills today may be exchanging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pay for them with a tomorrow/next value date," said Piotr Czyzewski, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at ING Bank in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  Demand at the T-bill tender totalled 4.7 billion zlotys against a 1.3 billion offer and all minimum prices rose for the first time in months. \par \par \uc2  "Good March inflation data and even better forecasts as well as the relative weakeness of the zloty make zloty instruments very attractive to foreigners," a T-bill dealer at a major bank here said. \par \par \uc2  Year-on-year inflation fell to 16.6 percent in March from 17.3 percent in February and is widely expected to drop to 13 percent in December. \par \par \uc2  On the money market O/N fell to 20.50/20.70 percent from 21.25 percent at opening while T/N eased to 20.70/21.00 percent from 21.40/21.60 percent despite a 699 million zloty net outflow after the NBP refrained all drainages. \par \par \uc2  "The high liquidity is puzzling, especially that tomorrow is the bulk of banks' monthly tax payments. But I expect rates may ease even further tomorrow," said Aleksandra Watroba of Bank Slaski SA. -- Jan Strupczewski +48 22 653 9700 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt4l06upy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
