\par\par \b \uc2  Polish zloty seen capped, yields up next week. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wojciech Moskwa \par \uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Feb 21 (Reuter) - The Polish zloty is likely to remain capped under strong resistance at three percent from the parity rate next week after battling repeated National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBP) \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week, dealers said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also said that recent price cuts in Polish treasuries would be continued at next Monday's primary T-bill auction, but they still expected demand to fall well short of the finance ministry's one billion zloty offer. \par \par \uc2  "The zloty will hold at three percent -- the level the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed it wants to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- with a downward drift possible if the NBP steps in again," said Bogdan Stepkowski, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at ING bank in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  Some said the NBP's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -buying cooled the market's pro-zloty sentiment enough that next week players could refrain from speculative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales, which had driven the zloty up in recent weeks along with foreign entry into Polish bonds. \par \par \uc2  "The zloty should trade between 2.7 and 3.0 percent from parity next week," said Jaroslaw Dryjski from Bank Slaski SA. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that foreign investors, who bought most of the 200 million zlotys worth of 12-percent fixed bonds sold on Thursday, were more likely to buy zlotys for their bonds at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's post-trade fixing than during trade. \par \par \uc2  "Many foreigners may buy zlotys at the fixing because it is safer, so trade may not be effected at all," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The value date for the fixed bonds, whose maximum yield amounted to 19.46 percent for just over four years, is March 5. \par \par \uc2  The zloty closed the week at 2.96 percent from the trading band centre, down from this week's high above 3.6 percent and last Friday's close at 3.17 percent. \par \par \uc2  It was at 3.0470 to the dollar, down from last Friday's 3.0310 and at 1.8090 to the mark, also down from 1.7985. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expected yields on all maturities to rise above 20 percent at Monday's T-bill tender after the finance ministry said it would increase yields to boost demand for treasuries. \par \par \uc2  "Everything should be above 20 percent with investor demand expected at up to 500 million zlotys," said Piotr Korcz, T-bill dealer at Bank Handlowy SA. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said foreigners would likely refrain from larger T-bill purchases until yields hit resistance in several weeks time. \par \par \uc2  They expected the average yield of the benchmark 52-week paper to rise above 20.20 percent from 20.07, with maximum yields hitting as high as 20.55 percent against this week's 20.40. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said money market one-day rates would likely hover around 22.5 percent next week as banks had surpluses on their required reserve accounts going into the last week of the February reserve, which ends on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "One-day cash should remain at present levels unless the NBP drains the market heavily as banks seem to have surpluses, but are reluctant to place cash at cheap rates," said Aleksandra Watroba, deposit dealer at Bank Slaski SA. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  drained more than one billion zlotys in reverse repos this week after not staging any reverse repo \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first two weeks of February. \par \par \uc2  Overnight rates closed the week at 22.30 percent and tomorrow/next rates at 22.35 percent. \par \par \uc2  -- Warsaw Newsroom +48 22 653 9700 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt2l09lom\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
